WEBVTT

1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:05,419
Welcome to EngageTrack, the engagement dashboard for the African Feminisms programme.

2
00:00:05,369 --> 00:00:13,547
It turns a complex, six-country evidence base into a clear view of reach, outcomes, organisational learning and lasting impact.

3
00:00:13,547 --> 00:00:18,857
From the funder overview, the revised August 2026 results are immediate.

4
00:00:18,857 --> 00:00:24,297
Three hundred and seventy-two women completed sixteen training cohorts across six countries.

5
00:00:24,297 --> 00:00:28,440
That is one hundred and five percent of the combined partner targets.

6
00:00:28,440 --> 00:00:36,261
Public events reached more than five hundred and seventeen people in person, with over fifty thousand reached through livestream and digital channels.

7
00:00:36,261 --> 00:00:39,751
All six organisational surveys are now complete.

8
00:00:39,751 --> 00:00:45,130
The outcomes view shows how EngageTrack protects the meaning behind the headline percentages.

9
00:00:45,130 --> 00:00:51,261
Programme results are weighted by valid participant responses, rather than averaging partner percentages.

10
00:00:51,261 --> 00:00:55,595
Knowledge increased for ninety-two point seven three percent of respondents.

11
00:00:55,595 --> 00:00:59,561
Confidence to advocate reached ninety-three point eight percent.

12
00:00:59,561 --> 00:01:05,285
Readiness to share learning and act as a community multiplier reached ninety point four eight percent.

13
00:01:05,285 --> 00:01:09,023
Every partner met every threshold on all three indicators.

14
00:01:09,023 --> 00:01:14,690
The partner comparison makes differences in context visible without losing the programme-wide story.

15
00:01:14,690 --> 00:01:21,107
Hawa Feminist Coalition received eight hundred and seventy-four applications for just one hundred and five places.

16
00:01:21,107 --> 00:01:27,226
Nearly half of participants were internally displaced, and twenty-one percent came from minority groups.

17
00:01:27,226 --> 00:01:33,071
Its hybrid model expanded access, while more than forty alumni moved into grassroots advocacy.

18
00:01:33,071 --> 00:01:39,239
Participation and inclusion are presented as confirmed evidence, not estimated demographics.

19
00:01:39,239 --> 00:01:45,595
In Mozambique, sign-language interpretation enabled women with hearing disabilities to participate fully.

20
00:01:45,595 --> 00:01:50,214
FEMIN-IN used Moore language facilitation with rural and displaced women.

21
00:01:50,214 --> 00:01:54,644
Pakasipiti centred LBQ women and queer feminist analysis.

22
00:01:54,644 --> 00:01:59,261
SustyVibes protected wellness and healing as part of feminist practice.

23
00:01:59,261 --> 00:02:03,668
EngageTrack also shows change inside the partner organisations.

24
00:02:03,668 --> 00:02:07,418
All six reported stronger planning and staff capacity.

25
00:02:07,418 --> 00:02:13,071
Five reported clearer goals, better donor reporting and stronger strategic decision-making.

26
00:02:13,071 --> 00:02:21,261
The lowest competency was linking financial expenditure to activities, making a dedicated financial monitoring session a clear next step.

27
00:02:21,261 --> 00:02:25,380
Most importantly, the dashboard connects data to decisions.

28
00:02:25,380 --> 00:02:36,559
Feedback added digital safety in Somalia, improved accessibility in Mozambique, changed delivery schedules in Zimbabwe, and helped inspire a feminist school in the Democratic Republic of Congo.

29
00:02:36,559 --> 00:02:45,380
Rede Elas Nos Media, the Lotus Community of Practice, active peer networks and alumni now leading later cohorts show how the investment continues.

30
00:02:45,380 --> 00:02:50,571
Reports can be exported with the same weighted results and source lineage shown on screen.

31
00:02:50,571 --> 00:02:59,726
EngageTrack helps funders and partners move from reporting activity to understanding impact: what changed, why it matters, and where support should go next.
